(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 26-year-old woman develops itchy follicular papules and pustules across the trunk and buttocks two days after using a hotel hot tub. Her face, palms and soles are spared. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Contact dermatitis from pool chemicals","Miliaria from heat and occlusive clothing","Pseudomonas folliculitis","Staphylococcal folliculitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the timing, the distribution and the sparing of face, neck, soles and palms all fit. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with atopic dermatitis of the flexures develops weeping honey-coloured crusting over the affected eczema. Which best describes the infection?","choices":["A primary bacterial infection arising in previously normal intact skin","A primary fungal infection of skin already damaged by another condition","A foreign body reaction within skin already damaged by another condition","A secondary bacterial infection of skin already damaged by another condition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bullous impetigo can secondarily invade pre-existing lesions such as eczema. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy reports that his acne is worse across the shoulders where his football pads sit. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Acnegenic mineral oil exposure from equipment maintenance products","Anabolic steroid use, which should be asked about in any athlete","Bacterial folliculitis from occlusive clothing worn during training","Acne mechanica, where pressure occludes the pilosebaceous follicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — shoulder pads, orthopaedic casts and helmets are the named examples. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who smokes has had painful nodules and draining sinuses in both axillae and the groin for two years, flaring five or six times a year, with meshlike scars between them. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Recurrent furunculosis of the axilla","Cutaneous Crohn's disease of the groin","Erythrasma of the axilla and the groin","Hidradenitis suppurativa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — typical lesions, apocrine distribution and recurrence more than twice in six months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 44-year-old bartender has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, with thickened discoloured nails and cuticles that have separated from the nail plate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Acute paronychia","Onychomycosis","Nail psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an inflammatory reaction to irritants over at least six weeks, with Candida albicans commonest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has honey-coloured crusted lesions around the nose and mouth that appeared four days after a scratch, and tender submandibular nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex of the perioral skin","Ecthyma of the face and perioral area","Non-bullous impetigo","Bullous impetigo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a honey-coloured adherent crust on the face with regional lymphadenopathy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has not cleared after two weeks of topical clindamycin. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Unroof a pustule for Gram stain and culture, since this is now a resistant case","Switch to a different topical antibiotic and review again in a further two weeks","Begin oral isotretinoin, since follicular disease unresponsive to topicals needs it","Reassure and stop treatment, since folliculitis resolves without any intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — culture, potassium hydroxide mount, nasal swab and biopsy are all available. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has axillary nodules and sinuses. The diagnosis of hidradenitis suppurativa is being considered. Which test is required to confirm it?","choices":["Punch biopsy of an active nodule, since the histology is what establishes the diagnosis","Wood's lamp examination of the axilla, looking for the coral-red fluorescence","None — it is a clinical diagnosis resting on lesions, distribution and recurrence","Bacterial culture of the draining material, since the causative organism guides treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is not usually required, though it shows follicular occlusion and apocrine gland destruction. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has been using topical tretinoin at night and benzoyl peroxide in the morning, but has begun applying them together and now has stinging and peeling. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Switch both to alternate weeks, because the combination raises photosensitivity risk","Separate the two by at least three hours, because applying them together irritates the skin","Stop the benzoyl peroxide permanently, because it inactivates topical tretinoin on contact","Continue both together and wash the face more often to remove the excess product"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that interval is specified in the patient education. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a painful swollen fingertip with grouped small vesicles at the lateral nail fold. Which test would settle the diagnosis?","choices":["Gram stain and culture, to identify the bacterial cause","Tzanck smear, to rule out herpetic whitlow","Potassium hydroxide preparation, to rule out candida","Wood's lamp examination, to rule out erythrasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Potassium hydroxide rules out candida and Gram stain identifies bacteria. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with folliculitis has failed topical mupirocin. Scraping and culture are being arranged. Which additional test excludes fungal folliculitis?","choices":["A punch biopsy of normal skin adjacent to an affected follicle","A potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair","A Wood's lamp examination looking for coral-red fluorescence","A Tzanck smear taken from an unroofed pustule on the thigh"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the specific test named for dermatophyte folliculitis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A parent asks whether antibiotics given earlier would have prevented their child's post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Antibiotics prevent it reliably if started within the first forty-eight hours of the rash","Antibiotics prevent it only when the causative organism has been confirmed by culture","Antibiotics worsen it, which is why topical therapy is preferred in young children","Antibiotics do not prevent it, because the immune activation usually precedes treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is stated explicitly for this complication. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has erythrasma confined to both inguinal folds. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical mupirocin ointment applied three times daily","Topical erythromycin or clindamycin","Oral erythromycin or clarithromycin","Topical miconazole cream applied twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral erythromycin or clarithromycin is used for widespread disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with longstanding athlete's foot develops erysipelas of the same leg. Which is the most appropriate additional management step?","choices":["Begin oral fluconazole, since the causative organism here is a yeast","Take no additional step, since the two conditions are entirely unrelated","Treat the tinea pedis, since it is the portal of entry and predicts recurrence","Begin nasal mupirocin, since staphylococcal carriage drives recurrent disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the risk factors for erysipelas. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has an axillary abscess that has not drained spontaneously. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Warm soaks and observation","Oral antibiotics on their own","Wide excision of the axilla","Surgical incision and drainage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If a lesion drains on its own, warm soaks with broad-spectrum antibiotics are used. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with cellulitis of the leg still has a fever of 38.6 degrees Celsius after fifty-four hours of oral antibiotics. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same antibiotic, since inflammation resolves over two weeks","Stop the antibiotic and observe, since the fever may be drug related","Add a topical antibiotic to the affected area alongside the oral one","Change the antimicrobial therapy, guided by the culture results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fever beyond forty-eight hours is the stated trigger for a change. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a single painful fluctuant nodule 8 mm across on the back of the neck with a single central opening. He is afebrile. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Incision and drainage with culture, plus an oral antibiotic because the lesion is over 5 mm","Warm compresses alone, since a single afebrile lesion never requires any antibiotic","Oral antibiotics alone, since incising a furuncle risks seeding the bloodstream","Wide surgical excision of the affected area to prevent the lesion recurring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a single lesion over 5 mm is one of the stated antibiotic indications. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with hidradenitis suppurativa asks what he can do himself to reduce flares. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Shower promptly after any hot tub use and check the disinfectant level in the water","Stop smoking, lose weight, avoid heat and constrictive clothing, and cleanse with an antibacterial wash","Avoid all water contact with the affected areas and apply a barrier ointment twice each day","Isolate from close contacts until treatment has been underway for at least forty-eight hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — smoking cessation is described as essential, and laser hair removal is also offered. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has cellulitis of the thigh with a central area of purulence. Which antibiotic choice is most appropriate?","choices":["Penicillin V, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic","Topical mupirocin, with oral cephalexin if lesions become widespread","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, clindamycin or linezolid","Dicloxacillin or cephalexin, with clindamycin if penicillin allergic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — purulent cellulitis prompts cover for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with erysipelas of the face has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Clindamycin, which is reserved for patients who report a penicillin allergy","Penicillin V, with symptomatic care, hydration and elevation of the affected part","Cephalexin, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole added if purulence develops later","Mupirocin ointment, with oral dicloxacillin if the lesions become widespread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man treated for erythrasma asks how to stop it returning. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Keep the area clean and dry, avoid excessive heat and moisture, and maintain a healthy body weight","Avoid sharing towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors with anyone else in the household","Stop smoking and avoid constrictive clothing over the affected skin at all times","Use a broad-spectrum sunscreen daily and cover the area with clothing outdoors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside good general hygiene. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has confirmed necrotizing fasciitis. Which combination of care is required?","choices":["Elevation with warm compresses and a narrow-spectrum oral antibiotic course","Aggressive surgical debridement, broad antibiotic cover and surgical intensive care admission","Broad intravenous antibiotics with observation on a general ward for forty-eight hours","Bedside incision and drainage followed by discharge on oral antibiotic therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cover aerobic Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms and anaerobes, with a team approach. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with cellulitis of the calf has an area that is tense, cyanotic and bronzed, and has not improved on antibiotics. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Compression bandaging and elevation, because venous congestion explains the colour","Surgical debridement, because devitalised tissue is not perfused so antibiotics cannot reach it","Escalation to intravenous vancomycin, because the organism is likely to be resistant","Continued oral antibiotics for a further week, because resolution can take two weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the pitfall the lecture names explicitly. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has open and closed comedones across the forehead and nose, with no papules or pustules. He has used only over-the-counter face wash. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy over a sixteen to twenty week course","A combined oral contraceptive pill to reduce ovarian androgen production","A topical retinoid, switching to azelaic or salicylic acid if not tolerated","An oral tetracycline with topical benzoyl peroxide added to limit resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is the guideline entry point for comedonal acne. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 70-year-old immunocompromised man has extensive cellulitis of the leg with systemic signs. Which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["Wood's lamp examination and a potassium hydroxide preparation of the skin","Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage and a plucked hair for dermatophytes","Blood cultures, punch biopsy, complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase","No investigations, since cellulitis is a clinical diagnosis in every patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with imaging considered for underlying fasciitis or osteomyelitis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with pseudofolliculitis barbae must remain clean-shaven for work. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Use a single or double blade at a mild angle, avoid lift-and-cut systems, and keep razors clean","Shave daily against the direction of growth so the hairs are cut below the skin surface","Use the closest-cutting multi-blade razor available to reduce the frequency of shaving","Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with chemical depilatories and laser hair removal as alternatives. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with hidradenitis suppurativa has draining sinuses that persist despite topical clindamycin and a mild steroid cream. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["A three-month course of oral doxycycline with topical benzoyl peroxide added","Dilute acetic acid compresses applied to the sinuses twice daily for a month","Topical mupirocin applied to the sinus openings three times daily for a week","Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide to decrease the size of the draining sinuses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral prednisone reduces inflammation and prevents future lesions. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with suspected necrotizing fasciitis is febrile and hypotensive. The surgical team is available now. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Proceed to surgical debridement, since tests and imaging must not delay intervention","Complete computed tomography of the limb before any operative decision is taken","Await blood culture results before committing the patient to an operation","Trial forty-eight hours of broad intravenous antibiotics and then reassess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is a surgical emergency with high mortality. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with diabetes has extensive folliculitis and a culture growing methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which oral options are appropriate?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid","Cephalexin, dicloxacillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, erythromycin base or clarithromycin","Doxycycline, minocycline or sarecycline for acne"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin and dicloxacillin cover the methicillin-sensitive organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis is being prepared for theatre. Which laboratory studies should be sent?","choices":["Complete blood count with differential, chemistry, arterial blood gas, urinalysis, and blood and tissue cultures","Complete blood count and inflammatory markers only, since the remaining studies add nothing to management","Antistreptolysin O titre and throat culture, since Group A streptococcus is a common cause","No laboratory studies at all, since none of them changes the operative decision here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are sent, but they must not delay the surgical intervention itself. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"}]};
